# infra/.env.example — dev defaults for the local compose stack.
#
# REQUIRED: the compose file carries no inline defaults — every setting
# resolves from the environment. Copy this file to infra/.env before the
# first `up`:
#
#     cp infra/.env.example infra/.env
#     cd infra && docker compose up --build
#
# infra/.env is git-ignored; this example file is tracked. These are
# LOCAL-DEV-ONLY values — never use them anywhere shared or in production.
# The dev database password is a matched set: POSTGRES_PASSWORD here must
# match the password embedded in the per-service DATABASE_URL literals that
# remain in infra/docker-compose.yml (those carry one value per service
# under a shared name, so they cannot come from a single .env entry).
#
# Two deliberate exceptions, also noted in CONTRIBUTING.md section 3:
#   * RUST_LOG — roleplay-service and tilemap-service used to carry
#     service-specific debug filters (info,roleplay_service=debug /
#     info,tilemap_service=debug). One .env value cannot hold both, so the
#     shared default here is `info`.
#   * KNOWLEDGE_GRAPH_BACKEND — the old example pinned this to `age`, but
#     the compose default was empty (infer from what is provisioned) and the
#     base stack provisions no AGE database. Pin `age` only when layering
#     the knowledge-pg overlay.
# JWT_SECRET must be at least 32 characters if set.
JWT_SECRET=loreweave_local_dev_jwt_secret_change_me_32chars

# ── Service secrets — dev defaults, live values below ──
# Fine for local dev; for any non-local deployment set all of these,
# or you're running on a secret anyone can read on GitHub.
INTERNAL_SERVICE_TOKEN=dev_internal_token
ADMIN_TOKEN_ISSUER_SECRET=dev_admin_issuer_secret_distinct_32chars!
ADMIN_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY=dev_admin_audit_hmac_key_at_least_32chars
CONFIRM_TOKEN_SIGNING_SECRET=loreweave_local_dev_confirm_signing_secret_change_me_32chars
AGENT_REGISTRY_VAULT_KEY=loreweave_local_dev_agent_registry_vault_key_change_me_32
LLM_PAYLOAD_ENCRYPTION_KEY=loreweave_local_dev_llm_payload_key_change_me_32c
MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=loreweave
MINIO_SECRET_KEY=loreweave_dev_minio_secret
# One pair rotates the server AND every client together: the server's
# MINIO_ROOT_USER/PASSWORD read these same two vars.

# ── WS-1.0 · diary/assistant encryption KEK (REQUIRED for any non-local deployment) ──
# Wraps each user's per-user data key; that key encrypts diary bodies, assistant chat and
# private KG facts. The value below is the PUBLIC dev default, which is
# equivalent to storing that content unencrypted — replace it for anything non-local.
#   Generate:  openssl rand -base64 48
DIARY_ENCRYPTION_KEY=loreweave_local_dev_diary_kek_change_me
#
# ⚠️ ROTATION IS A DATA-LOSS OPERATION IF YOU SKIP THIS. Every service unwraps a user's
# key by trying the active KEK, then each retired one. If you ever CHANGE
# DIARY_ENCRYPTION_KEY you MUST move the OLD value here (comma-separated) — otherwise every
# existing user's diary, assistant chat and facts become permanently unreadable. Set it on
# EVERY service (auth, chat, knowledge, worker-ai) at the same time.
DIARY_ENCRYPTION_KEYS_RETIRED=

# API base URL baked into the frontend static build at `docker compose build` time
# (Vite compile-time var — changing it later requires a rebuild, not just a restart).
# Leave unset/empty (the default): the browser calls relative /v1, which this
# container's own nginx.conf proxies to api-gateway-bff — works unchanged behind
# any origin (localhost, LAN IP, ngrok, a real domain). Only set this to an
# absolute URL for a non-proxied custom setup (frontend and gateway on different,
# unrelated origins).
VITE_API_BASE=

# Used in verification/reset emails and as the OAuth audience base for public MCP
# (mcp-public-gateway's MCP_RESOURCE_URL derives from this — see below). Must be
# the exact origin you open the UI at in the browser (e.g. your ngrok URL).
PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:5174

# Public MCP (external-agent entry, /mcp) OAuth resource identifier. MUST equal
# <PUBLIC_APP_URL>/mcp exactly (both sides sign/verify against this audience) —
# set it together with PUBLIC_APP_URL, same origin, just with /mcp appended.
# frontend/nginx.conf proxies /mcp -> api-gateway-bff -> mcp-public-gateway, so
# no separate port/domain is needed for MCP either.
MCP_RESOURCE_URL=http://localhost:5174/mcp

# Module 02 services default ports
# BOOK_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8205
# SHARING_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8206
# CATALOG_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8207

# Public URL browsers use to fetch MinIO objects (book covers, TTS/video output,
# chat attachments). frontend/nginx.conf proxies /media/ -> minio:9000, so for a
# single-port deployment (e.g. ngrok) set this to your public origin + /media,
# e.g. https://your-tunnel.ngrok-free.app/media. Must match VITE_API_BASE's origin.
MINIO_EXTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:9123

# ── T25a vector secondary (OD-2, 2026-08-12) ────────────────────────────────
# Unset = off, and off is byte-identical to pre-T25a: one database, reads and writes
# on Neo4j. Set it to turn on DUAL-WRITE (write both, read the primary) — the read
# cutover is a separate decision (T25) that needs the soak this enables.
# Requires the opt-in layer:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.knowledge-pg.yml up -d knowledge-pg
KNOWLEDGE_VECTOR_DB_URL=


# Which graph engine knowledge-service uses: `age` (Apache AGE on knowledge-pg) or
# `neo4j`. TIER: code default + this pin (§8.5, PO 2026-08-22). The code default is the
# claim; the pin is the operational fact, so a stack states its engine instead of
# inheriting one. The key was MISSING from this example entirely, which is how a stack
# could run an engine nobody had chosen.
#
# ── CHOOSING AN ENGINE: THE SWITCH IS WHICH COMPOSE FILES YOU LAYER ──────────────────
#
#   docker compose up -d                            -> Neo4j. The base stack has no AGE
#                                                      database in it at all.
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml \
#                  -f docker-compose.knowledge-pg.yml up -d
#                                                   -> Apache AGE. That overlay starts the
#                                                      instance AND points the service at it.
#
# You do not have to set this variable for either. The service resolves an unset (or empty)
# value from what the deployment has actually PROVISIONED: an installation with no
# `KNOWLEDGE_AGE_DB_URL` has no AGE database, so it keeps serving Neo4j. Set it only to
# STATE the choice, or to override the evidence.
#
# ── UPGRADING AN EXISTING INSTALLATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing to do. Keep running the base stack and you keep Neo4j, on your existing data,
# with or without this variable set. The previous compose forced `age` on everyone who
# chose nothing — including installations with no AGE database — and the result was not a
# crash but an EMPTY graph, which reads exactly like a book with no knowledge yet.
#
# ── MOVING FROM NEO4J TO AGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This is a DATA MIGRATION, not a switch, and the two halves are deliberately separate so
# you can do them in the safe order:
#
#   1. Layer the knowledge-pg overlay. AGE is now running and addressable, and reads still
#      come from Neo4j — layering a database is EVIDENCE, not a command, and the overlay
#      does not pin the backend for exactly this reason.
#   2. Set `KNOWLEDGE_GRAPH_BACKEND=neo4j` here while you migrate, so the inference cannot
#      move your reads the moment the DSN appears.
#   3. Migrate (`services/knowledge-service/app/db/migrations/neo4j_to_age.py`) and verify
#      the node/edge counts match. An empty AGE graph is indistinguishable from a healthy
#      one with nothing in it, so compare counts rather than checking that reads succeed.
#   4. Flip to `age` (or clear this variable and let the evidence decide).
#
# ── ADDING A DIFFERENT ENGINE LATER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The engines live in a registry (`app/db/graph_backend.py: BACKENDS`), not a hardcoded
# list. A new one is one entry plus a branch in `graph_store_provider.get_graph_store`.
# `kuzu` is registered as EVALUATION and will be REFUSED with its reason if you select it:
# `KuzuGraphStore` takes an open Kuzu connection rather than a `CypherSession`, so the
# provider has nothing to hand it. The refusal names that, so you get a sentence instead
# of a stack trace.
# KNOWLEDGE_GRAPH_BACKEND is deliberately left unset (no live line): empty
# means "infer from what is provisioned", and the base stack provisions no
# AGE database — and a live empty line also trips the port-adoption gate's
# declaration scanner, which reads the following line's key as the value.
# The old example pinned this to `age`; set `age` only when layering the
# knowledge-pg overlay.

# Where knowledge-service reads PASSAGE vectors from: `postgres` (pgvector on knowledge-pg)
# or `neo4j`. TIER: deploy ceiling — the compose default is `neo4j`, so a stack that says
# nothing here reads passages from the graph. That is not hypothetical: dev declares nothing,
# and on 2026-08-24 its `passage_embeddings_1024` showed readCount 4090 two days after the
# DDL that creates it had been deleted as "no reader left". The index survived as residue;
# the next rebuild would have made passage search a 500.
#
# `port-adoption-gate` counts every non-`postgres` declaration, and the passage vector DDL in
# `neo4j_schema.cypher` may be deleted only when that count reaches 0 (T25z, §9.2's coupling).
# Setting this to `postgres` requires the secondary to be POPULATED first — an empty secondary
# fails silently, which is §9.1's option 2 and the shape this plan has caught five times.
KNOWLEDGE_VECTOR_READ_PRIMARY=neo4j

# ── The rest — previously inline `${VAR:-default}` fallbacks ───────────────
# These had no entry in the old example at all: their dev defaults lived in
# infra/docker-compose.yml until the P0-1 change moved every default here.
# RUST_LOG is the shared default `info`; the two Rust services used to carry
# service-specific debug filters (see the header of this file).
ADMIN_JWT_LOCAL_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=
ADMIN_JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM=
AGENTRUNTIME_ARM=false
AGENT_REGISTRY_ALLOW_INTERNAL_MCP=1
